# SHA-256 of the certificate every published APK must be signed with.
#
# Not a secret — a certificate fingerprint is public by design. It lives here
# rather than in a repository secret so that changing the release key is a
# visible commit rather than a silent settings change, and so CI catches a
# build signed with the wrong key BEFORE it reaches dist/.
#
# What this exists to catch is the build minting a NEW key on every run, which
# is exactly what was happening while SHARECARD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 was unset: a
# fresh debug key per runner, so no published build could ever install over
# another, and Android's whole explanation was "App not installed".
#
# The key is EC secp256r1, alias "sharecard", valid until January 2054. The
# private half belongs in the SHARECARD_KEYSTORE_BASE64 repository secret and
# NOWHERE ELSE — not in this repository, and it must never be. If it is lost a
# new one can be generated, but everybody with the app installed has to
# uninstall before they can install again, so keep a backup of the secret's
# value somewhere that is not this repository.
#
# This check is skipped entirely while the secret is unset (see the workflow's
# have_key guard), so committing it ahead of the secret existing costs nothing.
